{-| The Unicode 17.0.0 property tables `core` answers from.

**Generated by `scripts/unicode-tables.py` in the `geng-lang` repository — do
not edit.** `docs/unicode.md` U2 is the rule these exist to keep — every
Unicode-dependent operation in `core` is answered from a pinned version rather
than from the host — and `docs/m1b-str.md` §T16 is where they were built.

Every table is a sorted array of ranges, stored flat, and every lookup in
`Unicode.Lookup` is a binary search over one of them.

  - `whitespaceRanges` and `nonPrintableRanges` are `[lo, hi, ...]`.
  - `whitespaceAscii` and `nonPrintableAscii` are the first 128 codepoints of
    those two, as a record of four 32-bit words — the same answer, reachable
    without a search and without allocating (§T16.2).
  - `upperRuns` and `lowerRuns` are `[lo, hi, delta, ...]`: every codepoint
    from `lo` to `hi` maps to itself plus `delta`. `upperAscii` and `lowerAscii`
    are the single run of each that covers ASCII, lifted out so that a letter
    does not pay for a search of the other 687 (§T17.2).
  - `upperMultiFrom` and `upperMultiTo` are the mappings that produce more than
    one codepoint, which no delta can express — `ß` to `SS` and its kind. They
    are `Array Char` and not `String` because `String` imports the lookups that
    read these tables, and a `String` here would close that circle.
  - `graphemeRanges` is `[lo, hi, class, ...]`, where a class packs the three
    properties UAX #29 asks about — see `graphemeClasses`, which is what the
    packing means. `graphemeControlAscii` is the ASCII half of it, which is
    three classes and a default, and `graphemeHangul` is the syllable block,
    which is arithmetic rather than a table (D162).

@docs whitespaceRanges, whitespaceAscii
@docs upperRuns, upperAscii, upperMultiFrom, upperMultiTo
@docs lowerRuns, lowerAscii, lowerMultiFrom, lowerMultiTo
@docs nonPrintableRanges, nonPrintableAscii
@docs graphemeRanges, graphemeClasses, graphemeControlAscii, graphemeHangul

-}

import Array exposing (Array)
import Basics exposing (Int)
import Char exposing (Char)


